     "text": "```python\ndef int_to_roman(num: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert an integer to a Roman numeral.\n\n    The function uses a mapping of integer values to their corresponding Roman numeral symbols,\n    and iteratively subtracts the largest possible value from the input number, appending the\n    corresponding symbol to the result string.\n\n    Args:\n        num (int): The integer to be converted to a Roman numeral.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The Roman numeral representation of the input integer.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input is not a positive integer between 1 and 3999 inclusive.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate input\n    if not isinstance(num, int) or num < 1 or num > 3999:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer between 1 and 3999 inclusive.\")\n\n    # Define the value-symbol pairs\n    val_to_symbol = [\n        (1000, 'M'),\n        (900, 'CM'),\n        (500, 'D'),\n        (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'),\n        (90, 'XC'),\n        (50, 'L'),\n        (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'),\n        (9, 'IX'),\n        (5, 'V'),\n        (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    # Initialize the result string\n    roman_num = ''\n\n    # Iterate through the value-symbol pairs\n    for value, symbol in val_to_symbol:\n        while num >= value:\n            roman_num += symbol\n            num -= value\n\n    return roman_num\n```",
